Q: &quot;no view found for id for fragment&quot; error before onCreateView() called A: <p>You're doing:</p>
<pre><code>setContentView(R.layout.activity_fragments_main);

FragmentTransaction ft = getSupportFragmentManager().beginTransaction();
ft.add(R.id.streamingactivity, new StreamingActivity());
ft.commit();
</code></pre>
<p>But <code>activity_fragments_main.xml</code> <strong>does not contain</strong> a view with the ID <code>@id/streamingactivity</code>.</p>
<p>It only contains a <code>TextView</code>:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;RelativeLayout ...&gt;

    &lt;TextView
        ...
        android:text=&quot;@string/hello_world&quot;/&gt;

&lt;/RelativeLayout&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>The <code>FrameLayout</code> with the ID <code>streamingactivity</code> exists in <strong>main.xml</strong>:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;FrameLayout
    android:id=&quot;@+id/streamingactivity&quot;
    android:layout_width=&quot;match_parent&quot;
    android:layout_height=&quot;match_parent&quot;/&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>However, <code>main.xml</code> is being inflated <strong>inside the fragment</strong> in <code>onCreateView()</code>. That layout is <strong>not</strong> part of the activity's view hierarchy when you call:</p>
<pre><code>fragmentTransaction.add(R.id.streamingactivity, streamingActivity);
</code></pre>
<p><code>FragmentTransaction.add()</code> expects the container ID to refer to a view that already exists in the <strong>activity's</strong> layout (the one passed to <code>setContentView()</code>), not a view that will later be created by the fragment itself.</p>
<p>The fix is to move the <code>FrameLayout</code> into <code>activity_fragments_main.xml</code>:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;RelativeLayout
    ...&gt;

    &lt;FrameLayout
        android:id=&quot;@+id/streamingactivity&quot;
        android:layout_width=&quot;match_parent&quot;
        android:layout_height=&quot;match_parent&quot;/&gt;

&lt;/RelativeLayout&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>Then let your fragment inflate only its own UI in <code>onCreateView()</code>.</p>
<p>In short:</p>
<ul>
<li><p><code>setContentView()</code> creates the activity's view hierarchy.</p>
</li>
<li><p><code>FragmentTransaction.add(containerId, fragment)</code> looks for <code>containerId</code> <strong>in that activity hierarchy</strong>.</p>
</li>
<li><p>Only after the container is found does Android call the fragment's <code>onCreateView()</code> to create the fragment's UI.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>That's why <code>onCreateView()</code> is never reached: the transaction fails first because the container view cannot be found.</p>
